# DF(1M)

## NAME

df - disk free

## SYNOPSIS

| **df** [ **-iw** ] [ filesystem | file ] ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Df* prints, for each file system, how much room it has and how much of it is gone. The report
is Berkeley's:

```
Filesystem  1K-blocks     Used    Avail Capacity  Mounted on
/dev/md0         5901     4140     1761    70%    /
```

With no argument the file systems reported on are **the root** and every one named in
*/etc/mtab*, which is the table *mount*(1M) and *umount*(1M) keep between them. An argument may
be **a special file** - block or character, and the block name is the one */etc/mtab* records -
**a mount point,** or **any ordinary file,** in which case the file system holding it is the
one reported on. A file that turns out to *hold* a file system, rather than to live on one, is
reported on directly; that is how a flat image can be handed to this program.

## THE COLUMNS

**1K-blocks**
: The **data area** of the volume: *s_fsize* less *s_isize*. Neither the super-block nor the
  i-list is in this column or in any other, so it is smaller than the volume.

**Used, Avail**
: *Avail* is the super-block's *s_tfree*; *Used* is the difference.

**Capacity**
: *Used* as a percentage of the two together. There is no Berkeley *minfree* here, so nothing is
  withheld from an ordinary user and *Avail* is what *Avail* says.

**iused, ifree, %iused**
: Printed only with **-i**. The total is the size of the i-list - *s_isize* less the
  super-block's own block, times *INOPB* - and *ifree* is *s_tinode*.

**Note:** *iused* counts **i-node 1,** which can never be allocated - *ialloc*() refuses
anything below *ROOTINO* - so a file system holding seven files reports eight used. That is
v7's arithmetic and it is kept.

## OPTIONS

**-i**
: Print the three i-node columns as well.

**-w**
: Get the free-block count by **walking the free list,** block by block through the chain blocks,
  exactly as the kernel's *alloc*() drains it - which is what *df* did in this port until now,
  and what v7's *df* does always.

**Note:** *s_tfree* is **maintained** by this kernel, which v7's is not: v7's own filsys(5)
calls the field uncurrent and its *df* therefore cannot read it. *Alloc*(), *free*(),
*ialloc*() and *ifree*() keep both totals here, so the default is one *lseek* rather than a
walk - and free *i-nodes* become askable at all, which no walk of the free list could have
answered.

**Note: -w is a check and not an alternative.** The two are readings of one number, so **df -w
must print exactly what df prints;** when they differ, *df* says so on the standard error and
prints the walked figure. **-l** is deliberately *not* the letter: every other *df* spells that
one "local file systems only".

**Note: -w** forces the **read** route for every argument, mounted or not, since the walk *is*
the super-block reader. On a mounted volume that means the raw device, and so **df -w** is the
super-user's where a bare *df* is not. That is the point of the flag rather than a limitation
of it: two readings that agreed by construction would be measuring nothing.

## BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES

**Note:** the block *df* counts in is **1024** bytes. It is not this machine's filesystem
block, which is **3072** bytes - 512 words of 48 bits - and is three of them.

Two things follow. Every number *df* prints is a **multiple of three,** the smallest thing that
can be allocated being one 3072-byte block: a one-byte file costs three. And the numbers are
**half** what a PDP-11 printed, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks - not a sixth, which is
what they were when this program counted in the filesystem block.

**Note:** there is no *BSHIFT* on this machine and there cannot be, 3072 not being a power of
two; the conversion is a divide by the filesystem block and then a multiply, and both live in
*sys/param.h*.

Du(1), *quot*(1M) and ls(1) report in the same block.

## WHO MAY RUN IT

There are **two routes** to the same six numbers, and one rule picks between them: **a mounted
filesystem is asked, a device or an image is read.**

**Asked** means statfs(2): the four counts come out of the kernel's **in-core** super block,
for the filesystem holding a path the caller can already name. No device is opened and **no
privilege is wanted,** so a bare *df*, *df /mnt* and *df /some/file* are ordinary user
commands.

**Read** means what this program has always done - open the raw twin, seek to the super block,
and obey the four alignment rules a raw transfer here has. *df /dev/rmd0*, *df image.img* and
**-w** take that route, because a raw device, an unmounted image and a walk of the free list
are not questions the kernel can be asked. Open(2) is the whole of the gate, and */dev/rmd0* is
mode 0600 because that one node is the contents of every file on the volume - so those three
forms are the super-user's, and *df* says so in those words rather than "cannot open".

**Note:** *df* is deliberately **not** set-user-id and never was: a set-uid *df* would hand the
whole filesystem to anyone able to think of an offset. What made the default report
unprivileged was not a change of mode or a borrowed user-id but statfs(2), which hands over the
counts without handing over the device.

**Note:** the asked route is also the **more current** of the two. *s_tfree* and *s_tinode*
live in core until *update*() writes them back, so the copy on the disk is stale by
construction - which is why the read route calls sync(2) first and the asked route makes no
such call.

## FILES

*/etc/mtab*
: the mounted-file-system table. A missing one is an empty table and not an error, so on a
  machine that has mounted nothing *df* reports the root alone.

*/dev/md0*
: the root, and the first entry of the default list. Given a **block** special file *df* opens
  the **raw** twin of the name - */dev/md1* is read through */dev/rmd1* - and falls back to the
  block device when there is no such node. There is one EC-5052 drive attached by an ordinary
  boot, of 2000 blocks, and there are no partitions; swap is on the drums.

## DIAGNOSTICS

**the raw device is the super-user's; name a file or a mount point** means the read route was
taken - a named device, an image, or **-w** - and open(2) refused it. The advice is the whole
of the fix: name the mount point or any file on the volume and the asked route answers without
privilege.

**cannot open** names a file *df* could neither stat(2) nor read; **cannot stat** one whose
filesystem statfs(2) would not report on; **not a mounted filesystem** an ordinary file whose
device is in no table. Either way *df* goes on to the next argument and exits 0, which is v7's
behaviour and is kept. A bad **flag** is different and exits 1.

**not a filesystem** and **filesystem geometry mismatch** mean the super-block read back is not
one this system could mount; *sbcheck*() refuses the same two and *fsck*(1M) refuses to repair
them.

**the free list has N blocks, the superblock says M** is **-w**'s disagreement, and means one
of the two is wrong about the volume.

**bad free count** and **bad free block** mean the free list is corrupt at the block named, and
a **-w** count is then a lower bound rather than an answer.

## SEE ALSO

